---
import { getRelativeLocaleUrl } from "astro:i18n";
import Layout from "@/layouts/Layout.astro";
import Header from "@/components/Header.astro";
import Breadcrumb from "@/components/Breadcrumb.astro";
import Main from "@/components/Main.astro";
import Footer from "@/components/Footer.astro";
import FlowDiagram from "@/components/FlowDiagram.astro";
import config from "@/config";

const locale = Astro.currentLocale ?? config.site.lang;

const snapshot = [
  {
    label: "Role",
    value: "Supply Chain & Logistics Excellence Trainee (SET)",
  },
  { label: "Company", value: "AB InBev · Budweiser brewing operations, Wuhan" },
  { label: "Timeline", value: "Mar–Dec 2025 · Concluded" },
  {
    label: "Scope",
    value: "0 → 1 · AI knowledge assistant + data governance",
  },
];

// The retrieval path, abstracted into a line-art content visual (see DESIGN.md).
const flowSteps = [
  { label: "Plain-language question", note: "floor · desktop or mobile" },
  {
    label: "Hybrid retrieval (RAG)",
    note: "structured KB · plant procedures",
    emphasis: true,
  },
  { label: "Live operational data", note: "MES integration" },
  {
    label: "Standardized answer",
    note: "consistent, trustworthy format",
    emphasis: true,
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title={`AI-SOP Assistant at AB InBev (0 → 1) — ${config.site.title}`}
  description="A concluded AB InBev case study: an on-prem RAG assistant for plant procedures and maintenance, with IoT governance, Grafana, and MES integration."
  ogImage="/og/ai-sop-assistant.png"
>
  <Header />
  <Breadcrumb />

  <Main pageTitle="AI-SOP Assistant at AB InBev (0 → 1)" class="app-prose">
    <p class="text-muted-foreground text-sm">
      <a
        href={getRelativeLocaleUrl("zh", "work/ai-sop-assistant")}
        class="text-accent hover:underline"
      >
        阅读中文版
      </a>
    </p>

    <p class="text-muted-foreground text-lg">
      At AB InBev's Budweiser brewing operations in Wuhan, I built an internal
      AI assistant that let frontline logistics and engineering staff ask a
      question in plain language and get back a standardized answer drawn from
      the plant's own procedures and live operational data. This is a concluded
      0 → 1 project; the page describes the problem, how I approached it, and
      what I carry forward — without exposing operational specifics.
    </p>

    <div class="border-border mt-4 flex flex-wrap gap-2">
      <span
        class="text-foreground rounded-full border px-3 py-1 text-xs font-medium"
      >
        Case Study · Concluded Dec 2025
      </span>
      <span
        class="text-foreground rounded-full border px-3 py-1 text-xs font-medium"
      >
        RAG
      </span>
      <span
        class="text-foreground rounded-full border px-3 py-1 text-xs font-medium"
      >
        Local LLM
      </span>
      <span
        class="text-foreground rounded-full border px-3 py-1 text-xs font-medium"
      >
        Data Governance
      </span>
      <span
        class="text-foreground rounded-full border px-3 py-1 text-xs font-medium"
      >
        Industrial AI
      </span>
    </div>

    <div
      class="border-border bg-border mt-6 grid gap-px overflow-hidden rounded-lg border sm:grid-cols-2"
    >
      {
        snapshot.map(item => (
          <div class="bg-background p-4">
            <p class="text-muted-foreground text-xs font-medium tracking-wide uppercase">
              {item.label}
            </p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-sm">{item.value}</p>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>

    <div
      class="border-border bg-muted/30 text-muted-foreground mt-6 rounded-lg border p-4 text-sm"
    >
      <strong class="text-foreground">A note on confidentiality.</strong> This was
      real work inside a brewing operation. I describe the problem space, my methodology,
      and the named open tools I used; plant-layout and operational specifics, production
      data, and internal system names stay out. Outcomes are directional and reflect
      what was measured during my tenure.
    </div>

    <!-- ─────────────────────────  1. CONTEXT  ───────────────────────── -->
    <h2 class="mt-8 text-xl font-semibold">Context</h2>
    <p>
      Two problems kept surfacing. First, when equipment failed, the response
      was slow: the knowledge needed to diagnose and act was scattered across
      documents and people, so the clock ran while someone went looking. Second,
      standard operating procedures were hard to find — an operator or engineer
      who needed the right SOP had to hunt across logistics and engineering
      departments that each kept their own material.
    </p>
    <p>
      A brewery is an unforgiving place to solve this with a generic chatbot.
      The data can't leave the premises, the users are on the floor rather than
      at a desk, and the answers have to line up with legacy systems that
      already run the plant. Those three constraints — data residency, frontline
      users, and existing systems — shaped every decision that followed.
    </p>

    <!-- ─────────────────────────  2. DISCOVERY  ───────────────────────── -->
    <h2 class="mt-8 text-xl font-semibold">Discovery</h2>
    <p>
      I didn't start from a model. I rotated through production, logistics, and
      engineering, decomposed how work actually happened, and interviewed the
      people who'd use the thing. What came out of that was less glamorous than
      a demo suggests: people didn't want a conversational partner, they wanted
      the right procedure, fast, in a form they could trust and act on. That
      reframed the product from "a chatbot for the plant" into "the shortest
      reliable path from a question to the correct, standardized answer."
    </p>

    <!-- ─────────────────────────  3. WHAT I BUILT  ───────────────────────── -->
    <h2 class="mt-8 text-xl font-semibold">What I built</h2>

    <h3 class="mt-6 text-lg font-medium">Knowledge architecture</h3>
    <p>
      I built a structured knowledge base from the plant's full procedure and
      maintenance corpus. That meant defining chunking rules, annotation
      standards, and a taxonomy so retrieval had something clean to work against
      — the unglamorous data work that decides whether an answer is right.
    </p>

    <h3 class="mt-6 text-lg font-medium">Retrieval flow</h3>
    <p>
      The flow itself is simple to state: a natural-language question goes
      through hybrid retrieval and comes back in a standardized answer format.
      The standardization mattered as much as the retrieval — a consistent shape
      is what let frontline users trust the output and act on it without
      second-guessing.
    </p>

    <FlowDiagram
      steps={flowSteps}
      caption="The assistant's path, abstracted: a plain-language question runs through hybrid RAG retrieval over the structured knowledge base, is grounded against live operational data via the MES, and returns in a standardized answer format."
    />

    <h3 class="mt-6 text-lg font-medium">Local model deployment</h3>
    <p>
      Because the data couldn't leave the site, I coordinated the on-premise
      deployment of an open-source model — DeepSeek — rather than reaching for a
      hosted API. It shipped as Streamlit apps on both desktop and mobile, so
      the assistant met people where they worked instead of tethering them to a
      workstation.
    </p>

    <h3 class="mt-6 text-lg font-medium">IoT data governance</h3>
    <p>
      In parallel I led end-to-end data governance for the plant's IoT sensor
      fleet: collection, cleaning, format standardization, and quality
      validation, turning a noisy sensor stream into trustworthy time-series
      data assets. On top of that data I built Grafana dashboards for energy
      management and predictive-maintenance views that plant management used.
    </p>

    <h3 class="mt-6 text-lg font-medium">Live-data integration</h3>
    <p>
      Finally, I drove the integration between the assistant and the plant's
      MES, so answers reflected live operational data rather than a static
      snapshot of documents. A procedure is only as useful as its grounding in
      what the plant is actually doing right now.
    </p>

    <!-- ─────────────────────────  4. DECISIONS  ───────────────────────── -->
    <h2 class="mt-8 text-xl font-semibold">Decisions worth recording</h2>

    <h3 class="mt-6 text-lg font-medium">
      Local deployment over API convenience
    </h3>
    <p>
      The obvious fast path was a hosted API. I chose on-premise open-source
      deployment instead, because in a brewing operation data residency isn't a
      preference, it's a constraint — and a tool that can't be adopted for
      compliance reasons is worth nothing, however good its answers.
    </p>

    <h3 class="mt-6 text-lg font-medium">
      Annotation and maintenance as first-class work
    </h3>
    <p>
      I spent real effort on things that don't demo well: annotation standards,
      chunk-splitting rules, and an update cycle, written up as a maintenance
      SOP alongside a Data Governance Standard. A knowledge base decays the day
      its author leaves unless the upkeep is defined — I wanted the system to
      outlive me on the team.
    </p>

    <h3 class="mt-6 text-lg font-medium">
      A standardized answer over open-ended chat
    </h3>
    <p>
      I constrained the output to a standardized format rather than free-form
      conversation. Frontline users don't need eloquence; they need an answer
      shaped the same way every time, so they can trust it and act on it under
      time pressure.
    </p>

    <!-- ─────────────────────────  5. OUTCOMES  ───────────────────────── -->
    <h2 class="mt-8 text-xl font-semibold">Outcomes</h2>
    <p>
      After rollout, the assistant saved hundreds of engineering hours each
      month; knowledge reuse rose sharply and rework fell dramatically as the
      right procedure became easy to find and consistent to follow. These are
      directional figures as measured during my tenure — I don't publish exact
      internal metrics from a former employer.
    </p>

    <!-- ─────────────────────────  6. LESSONS  ───────────────────────── -->
    <h2 class="mt-8 text-xl font-semibold">What it taught me</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Governance isn't a feature you add later.</strong> The hardest part
      of this project wasn't retrieval; it was making the data and the answers trustworthy
      enough that people on the floor would rely on them. That's why I now build governance-first
      agent platforms — the reliability work is the product, not the polish.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>The knowledge layer is the moat.</strong> Chunking, annotation, taxonomy,
      upkeep — the boring layer decided whether the AI was right. I carry that discipline
      into every system I design now, and it's the same instinct behind my ontology
      work.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Meet users where they are.</strong> A brewery taught me that a great
      answer at a desk is useless to someone on the floor. Delivery format is part
      of the product, not an afterthought.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Build for handover from day one.</strong> Writing the maintenance SOP
      was the moment the tool became infrastructure instead of a personal project.
      A system that can't survive its builder leaving was never finished.
    </p>

    <div
      class="border-border bg-muted/30 mt-8 flex flex-col gap-3 rounded-lg border p-5 sm:flex-row sm:items-center sm:justify-between"
    >
      <div>
        <p class="font-medium">This is where the platform work comes from</p>
        <p class="text-muted-foreground text-sm">
          What I'm building now applies these lessons at platform scale.
        </p>
      </div>
      <a
        href={getRelativeLocaleUrl(locale, "work/enterprise-agent-platform")}
        class="bg-accent text-accent-foreground inline-flex shrink-0 items-center gap-1.5 rounded-md px-5 py-2.5 text-sm font-medium transition-opacity hover:opacity-90"
      >
        See Current Work &rarr;
      </a>
    </div>

    <div class="border-border mt-8 rounded-lg border p-6">
      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold">Related</h2>
      <div class="mt-3 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
        <a
          href={getRelativeLocaleUrl(locale, "work/enterprise-agent-platform")}
          class="text-accent hover:underline"
        >
          Enterprise Agent Platform
        </a>
        <a
          href={getRelativeLocaleUrl(locale, "projects/prompt-to-ontology")}
          class="text-accent hover:underline"
        >
          Prompt-to-Ontology
        </a>
        <a
          href={getRelativeLocaleUrl(locale, "playbook/harness-engineering")}
          class="text-accent hover:underline"
        >
          Harness Engineering
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Main>

  <Footer />
</Layout>
